1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,535
Your CTO asks: "Half the team hides local
configuration edits with git update-index

2
00:00:09,575 --> 00:00:14,549
--assume-unchanged. Last week a pull was
refused on a clean working tree, and then

3
00:00:14,589 --> 00:00:19,073
somebody's settings were gone. Why is
there no switch for 'ignore my local

4
00:00:19,113 --> 00:00:21,947
changes'?" Think about
it, and say your answer.

5
00:00:26,787 --> 00:00:31,452
Because Git has no such feature. The bit
that the team uses is a promise that you

6
00:00:31,492 --> 00:00:34,995
will not edit the file. It's not
a request to overlook edits.

7
00:00:35,335 --> 00:00:40,336
That answer comes from reading the index
entry by entry, which is what we do today.

8
00:00:40,376 --> 00:00:45,378
The index is Git's list of what goes into
the next commit, one entry for each file.

9
00:00:45,418 --> 00:00:50,359
An entry has more in it than a path and a
blob ID, the ID of the stored content: a

10
00:00:50,399 --> 00:00:54,247
stage number, flag bits, and cached
data about the file on disk.

11
00:00:54,587 --> 00:00:57,576
Each of those fields explains
a class of surprises.

12
00:00:57,616 --> 00:01:02,030
And the CTO's story, the refusal on a
clean tree and the vanished settings?

13
00:01:02,070 --> 00:01:03,931
You'll watch both halves happen.

14
00:01:05,771 --> 00:01:09,400
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

15
00:01:09,440 --> 00:01:14,151
This is the last video on the index, and
it's the one for the person who has to

16
00:01:14,191 --> 00:01:14,993
repair things.

17
00:01:15,333 --> 00:01:20,403
We go through one index entry, field by
field. git ls-files, the scripting view.

18
00:01:20,443 --> 00:01:24,364
The two flag bits that people
mistake for an ignore mechanism.

19
00:01:24,404 --> 00:01:27,941
The stage number, as a short
preview of merge conflicts.

20
00:01:27,981 --> 00:01:32,732
The cached stat data, which explains a
"modified" file that hasn't changed.

21
00:01:32,772 --> 00:01:36,884
And the index as a file on disk:
its lock, and how to rebuild it.

22
00:01:38,724 --> 00:01:40,302
Here's what you'll walk away with.

23
00:01:40,642 --> 00:01:42,054
After this video you can:

24
00:01:42,394 --> 00:01:46,797
List index entries, untracked files
and ignored files with git ls-files.

25
00:01:47,137 --> 00:01:53,254
Explain why --assume-unchanged and
--skip-worktree are not ways to ignore local

26
00:01:53,294 --> 00:01:53,877
changes.

27
00:01:54,217 --> 00:01:57,927
Name the three stages an index
entry has during a conflict.

28
00:01:58,267 --> 00:02:02,336
Explain a false "modified" from the
cached stat data, and repair it.

29
00:02:02,676 --> 00:02:06,603
Diagnose a stale index.lock,
and rebuild a corrupt index.

30
00:02:08,443 --> 00:02:13,213
git ls-files. In one sentence: git ls-files
is the plumbing view of the index.

31
00:02:13,253 --> 00:02:18,084
Plumbing means the low-level commands
made for scripts, and porcelain means the

32
00:02:18,124 --> 00:02:23,264
everyday commands made for people. It lists
entries and, with options, compares them

33
00:02:23,304 --> 00:02:28,320
with the working tree, the files on your
disk, without the interpretation that git

34
00:02:28,360 --> 00:02:29,923
status adds. It is 🟢 SAFE.

35
00:02:30,263 --> 00:02:36,364
No option, or -c: every path in the index.
-s, --stage: mode, blob ID, stage number

36
00:02:36,404 --> 00:02:42,579
and path of every entry. -m and -d: tracked
paths whose file differs from the entry,

37
00:02:42,619 --> 00:02:47,685
where a deleted file counts, and
tracked paths whose file is missing.

38
00:02:48,025 --> 00:02:55,350
-o, --others: paths with no entry. Ignore
rules apply only with --exclude-standard.

39
00:02:55,390 --> 00:03:01,384
-i, --ignored: with -o, untracked
paths that an ignore rule matches.

40
00:03:01,424 --> 00:03:08,749
With -c, tracked paths that one matches.
-v adds a status tag, and --error-unmatch,

41
00:03:08,789 --> 00:03:11,411
--format and -z serve scripts.

42
00:03:11,751 --> 00:03:16,861
Two bits that are not an ignore mechanism.
In one sentence: git update-index

43
00:03:16,901 --> 00:03:22,484
--assume-unchanged and --skip-worktree each
set a flag on an index entry that tells

44
00:03:22,524 --> 00:03:28,176
Git not to examine the working-tree file,
and neither is a way to keep private edits

45
00:03:28,216 --> 00:03:33,122
to a tracked file. Both are 🟡 CAUTION:
a flag bit that hides later edits.

46
00:03:33,462 --> 00:03:38,278
Both flags are documented for other jobs.
In the picture, think of them as telling

47
00:03:38,318 --> 00:03:40,172
Git not to look at the left box.

48
00:03:40,512 --> 00:03:45,526
--assume-unchanged: its documented purpose
is speed on filesystems with a slow

49
00:03:45,566 --> 00:03:50,257
lstat, the call that asks the filesystem
about a file without reading it.

50
00:03:50,297 --> 00:03:55,116
"The user promises not to change the
file". Its tag in git ls-files -v is a

51
00:03:55,156 --> 00:03:59,328
lower-case letter, h. git add on
the path does nothing, silently.

52
00:03:59,368 --> 00:04:04,318
A merge that changes the file is refused:
local changes would be overwritten.

53
00:04:04,358 --> 00:04:08,465
And git restore on the path
overwrites the edit without warning.

54
00:04:08,805 --> 00:04:13,718
--skip-worktree: its documented purpose
is sparse checkout, a working tree that

55
00:04:13,758 --> 00:04:18,735
holds only part of the project. "Avoid
writing the file to the working directory

56
00:04:18,775 --> 00:04:23,877
when reasonably possible". Its tag is S.
git add is refused with a sparse-checkout

57
00:04:23,917 --> 00:04:28,016
message. A merge that changes the
file is refused in the same way.

58
00:04:28,056 --> 00:04:31,339
git restore is refused: the
pathspec matches nothing.

59
00:04:31,679 --> 00:04:36,384
The FAQ answers the underlying wish directly:
"How do I ignore changes to a tracked

60
00:04:36,424 --> 00:04:41,071
file? Git doesn't provide a way to do this",
and the two bits "don't work properly

61
00:04:41,111 --> 00:04:43,872
for this purpose and
shouldn't be used this way".

62
00:04:44,212 --> 00:04:47,847
Inside .git: one bit in one
entry of your .git/index.

63
00:04:47,887 --> 00:04:53,602
No commit, ref or configuration file records
it, so no clone has it, and rebuilding

64
00:04:53,642 --> 00:04:54,988
the index erases it.

65
00:04:55,328 --> 00:04:59,070
The textbook attaches a caveat,
and I pass it on as it stands.

66
00:04:59,110 --> 00:05:02,180
Do not read "refused" in
that table as a guarantee.

67
00:05:02,220 --> 00:05:07,304
Git decides whether a flagged file still
matches its entry from the stat data cached

68
00:05:07,344 --> 00:05:11,573
in the index, size and timestamps,
not by reading the file every time.

69
00:05:11,613 --> 00:05:16,513
Two exercise authors each saw, once, a
merge or pull overwrite a hidden edit that

70
00:05:16,553 --> 00:05:21,149
had the same size as the committed file
and was made within the same second.

71
00:05:21,489 --> 00:05:25,710
The run could not be reproduced on
demand, so no transcript is shown.

72
00:05:25,750 --> 00:05:30,897
The refusal is a best effort that depends
on the cached stat data, which is one more

73
00:05:30,937 --> 00:05:33,553
reason not to hide edits
behind either bit.

74
00:05:33,893 --> 00:05:38,808
What works instead. The arrangement the FAQ
recommends: shared defaults in a tracked

75
00:05:38,848 --> 00:05:43,527
file, private overrides in an ignored file
that no commit on any branch has ever

76
00:05:43,567 --> 00:05:46,123
tracked, and an application
that reads both.

77
00:05:46,463 --> 00:05:51,855
Stages. In one sentence: when a merge can't
decide the content of a path, the index

78
00:05:51,895 --> 00:05:57,222
holds up to three entries for it, at stages
1, 2 and 3, and resolving the conflict

79
00:05:57,262 --> 00:06:02,719
means replacing them with one entry at stage
0. A merge combines two branches, and a

80
00:06:02,759 --> 00:06:06,253
conflict is a spot where Git
can't combine them alone.

81
00:06:06,593 --> 00:06:11,641
From the manual: "During a merge, stage
1 is the common ancestor, stage 2 is the

82
00:06:11,681 --> 00:06:16,857
target branch's version (typically the
current branch), and stage 3 is the version

83
00:06:16,897 --> 00:06:19,338
from the branch which is being merged".

84
00:06:19,678 --> 00:06:23,102
The notation :<n>:<path>
names the blob at stage n.

85
00:06:23,142 --> 00:06:28,808
Take two facts into Part 2. An index with
unmerged entries can't be written out as a

86
00:06:28,848 --> 00:06:33,155
tree, so git commit refuses until
every path is back at stage 0.

87
00:06:33,195 --> 00:06:38,386
And git add doesn't judge a resolution:
it stages the file as it is, conflict

88
00:06:38,426 --> 00:06:40,899
markers included if you left them in.

89
00:06:41,239 --> 00:06:45,924
The cached stat data. In one sentence:
each index entry remembers the size and

90
00:06:45,964 --> 00:06:51,012
timestamps that the file had when Git last
saw it match the entry, so "has this file

91
00:06:51,052 --> 00:06:55,858
changed?" normally costs one lstat call
instead of reading and hashing the file.

92
00:06:56,198 --> 00:07:00,016
Matching data mean "unchanged". A
mismatch means "possibly changed".

93
00:07:00,056 --> 00:07:04,612
Here plumbing and porcelain differ. Plumbing
commands report the path as changed.

94
00:07:04,652 --> 00:07:09,095
Porcelain commands first refresh the
index: they re-read the file and, when the

95
00:07:09,135 --> 00:07:12,215
content still equals the blob,
store the new stat data.

96
00:07:12,255 --> 00:07:15,563
git update-index --refresh 🟢
SAFE does the same on request.

97
00:07:15,903 --> 00:07:21,184
The lock, and a rebuild. In one sentence:
every writer creates .git/index.lock,

98
00:07:21,224 --> 00:07:25,765
writes a complete new index into
it, and renames it over .git/index.

99
00:07:25,805 --> 00:07:31,288
A leftover lock therefore blocks all writers,
and a damaged index can be discarded

100
00:07:31,328 --> 00:07:36,745
and rebuilt from HEAD, the last commit,
at the price of whatever was only staged.

101
00:07:37,085 --> 00:07:41,483
A lock without a process means a
crash, a kill -9 or a power loss.

102
00:07:41,523 --> 00:07:46,460
A lock with a live process is normal:
an editor, a hook, another terminal.

103
00:07:46,500 --> 00:07:51,975
Removing a lock is 🟡 CAUTION: it's unsafe
while a Git process is writing, so check

104
00:07:52,015 --> 00:07:53,656
with pgrep -fl git first.

105
00:07:53,996 --> 00:07:57,761
Rebuilding is 🔴 DANGEROUS: rm
.git/index, then git reset.

106
00:07:57,801 --> 00:08:03,368
The five questions. What it changes: it
discards the index and writes a new one from

107
00:08:03,408 --> 00:08:08,909
HEAD's tree. What it can destroy: the staging
state, the flag bits and the conflict

108
00:08:08,949 --> 00:08:14,316
stages. That is, the record of what was
staged and not committed, which no reflog

109
00:08:14,356 --> 00:08:14,716
holds.

110
00:08:15,056 --> 00:08:20,458
How to preview: git diff --cached. And move
the file aside instead of deleting it.

111
00:08:20,498 --> 00:08:25,436
How to recover: git fsck for blobs that
were staged, or move the file back.

112
00:08:25,476 --> 00:08:30,944
When it is appropriate: only for a corrupt
index. And never during a merge, because

113
00:08:30,984 --> 00:08:33,864
the conflict stages
exist only in the index.

114
00:08:35,704 --> 00:08:40,482
The textbook's analogy for the stat cache:
a librarian who checks whether a book was

115
00:08:40,522 --> 00:08:43,981
touched by looking at the dust
on it instead of rereading it.

116
00:08:44,021 --> 00:08:47,136
Disturbed dust only means
that the book must be reread.

117
00:08:47,176 --> 00:08:51,839
It breaks at one point: Git knows when dust
can lie, namely an edit that keeps the

118
00:08:51,879 --> 00:08:56,371
size and lands in the same timestamp tick
as the index write, and rereads then.

119
00:08:56,711 --> 00:09:01,453
Use the same picture for the two bits.
Setting --assume-unchanged is telling the

120
00:09:01,493 --> 00:09:06,355
librarian "do not even look at the dust on
this book; I promise I have not touched

121
00:09:06,395 --> 00:09:11,257
it". If you then write in the book, the
catalogue is wrong, and every command that

122
00:09:11,297 --> 00:09:13,528
trusts the catalogue is wrong with it.

123
00:09:15,368 --> 00:09:17,402
Now one entry, with all its fields.

124
00:09:17,742 --> 00:09:21,458
Six fields. The first three are
what the commit's tree receives.

125
00:09:21,498 --> 00:09:26,095
The last three never leave your machine.
Every topic of this video lives in the

126
00:09:26,135 --> 00:09:26,800
bottom half.

127
00:09:27,140 --> 00:09:32,240
That's the CTO's story as a root-cause box.
Read the line marked root cause: the bit

128
00:09:32,280 --> 00:09:34,382
is a promise that there is no edit.

129
00:09:36,222 --> 00:09:41,033
The repository has a modified file, a
deleted file, an untracked file, two ignored

130
00:09:41,073 --> 00:09:44,582
ones, and an .env that was
committed before its ignore rule.

131
00:09:56,722 --> 00:10:02,117
--others alone lists ignored files too,
which is why --exclude-standard appears in

132
00:10:02,157 --> 00:10:07,354
nearly every real use. The last command
of this snippet is the detector for the

133
00:10:07,394 --> 00:10:09,541
already-tracked trap of video 12.

134
00:10:19,681 --> 00:10:24,134
Is this path tracked? The exit
status of --error-unmatch answers.

135
00:10:24,474 --> 00:10:29,722
git update-index --assume-unchanged 🟡
CAUTION, and then a private edit that points

136
00:10:29,762 --> 00:10:35,010
the service at a local model server. Predict
what git status says about that edit:

137
00:10:35,050 --> 00:10:37,331
modified, or clean? Say it out loud.

138
00:10:42,171 --> 00:10:48,018
Clean. git ls-files -v shows the bit as a
lower-case tag, and status prints nothing.

139
00:10:48,058 --> 00:10:53,484
Now a teammate's change to the same file
arrives. Predict what git merge does.

140
00:10:53,524 --> 00:10:55,377
Say it out loud. I'll wait.

141
00:11:00,217 --> 00:11:03,336
A refused merge and a clean
status at the same time.

142
00:11:03,376 --> 00:11:07,833
That's the first half of the CTO's
story. Then git restore on that path: 🔴

143
00:11:07,873 --> 00:11:12,816
DANGEROUS, as always, and here worse,
because it replaces the hidden edit with the

144
00:11:12,856 --> 00:11:17,131
index version, silently. That's the
second half: the settings are gone.

145
00:11:17,171 --> 00:11:20,716
You can't preview it with git
diff until you clear the bit.

146
00:11:28,056 --> 00:11:32,046
--skip-worktree 🟡 CAUTION fails
differently and no better.

147
00:11:32,386 --> 00:11:37,169
Find paths that carry either bit, then
clear them. The edit is visible again.

148
00:11:37,509 --> 00:11:42,613
Try it now, thirty seconds. In a repository
you work in, run git ls-files -v.

149
00:11:42,653 --> 00:11:47,890
It only reads. Look at the first letter
of each line: a lower-case letter, or a

150
00:11:47,930 --> 00:11:52,967
capital S, means one of the two bits is
set. Pause me, and note your answer.

151
00:11:57,807 --> 00:12:03,311
And the arrangement that works: shared
defaults tracked, private overrides in a file

152
00:12:03,351 --> 00:12:05,423
that no commit has ever tracked.

153
00:12:05,763 --> 00:12:07,898
Two branches changed the same line.

154
00:12:08,238 --> 00:12:13,829
One path, three entries, three blob IDs.
Quick quiz: which stage holds the version

155
00:12:13,869 --> 00:12:18,017
from your current branch? One,
two or three? Say your answer.

156
00:12:22,857 --> 00:12:26,862
Stage 2. Stage 1 is the merge
base, the common ancestor.

157
00:12:26,902 --> 00:12:31,628
Stage 2 is the current branch.
Stage 3 is the branch being merged.

158
00:12:31,668 --> 00:12:35,672
Each can be read as a complete
file. That's all for now.

159
00:12:35,712 --> 00:12:36,972
Part 2 lives here.

160
00:12:39,812 --> 00:12:44,466
The stat fields belong to your machine
and your clock, so this transcript masks

161
00:12:44,506 --> 00:12:45,654
their values with N.

162
00:12:45,994 --> 00:12:49,036
The script changes the
modification time of a file.

163
00:12:49,076 --> 00:12:52,359
The content stays the same.
Predict what plumbing says.

164
00:12:52,699 --> 00:12:57,057
git diff-files reports M. The all-zero
ID on the right is its notation for a

165
00:12:57,097 --> 00:13:00,125
working-tree file that is
out of sync with the index.

166
00:13:00,465 --> 00:13:05,385
git status printed nothing: it re-read the
file, found the same content, and wrote

167
00:13:05,425 --> 00:13:09,681
the new stat data back, so the second
git diff-files is silent as well.

168
00:13:10,021 --> 00:13:14,303
A real edit: refresh can't make this
entry match, and says "needs update".

169
00:13:17,143 --> 00:13:21,169
A release script asks plumbing
whether anything differs from HEAD.

170
00:13:21,209 --> 00:13:25,975
Then the repository is copied, as a build
context or a restored CI cache does.

171
00:13:26,015 --> 00:13:28,994
No file content changes,
and the copy is "dirty".

172
00:13:29,334 --> 00:13:31,482
Refresh first, and the check passes.

173
00:13:34,322 --> 00:13:38,793
The script imitates a Git process that
died while it was writing the index.

174
00:13:38,833 --> 00:13:40,657
Readers work, and writers fail.

175
00:13:40,997 --> 00:13:46,187
One staged change and one unstaged change
exist, and then the index file is damaged.

176
00:13:46,227 --> 00:13:50,670
The five questions for the rebuild
were answered in the concept segment.

177
00:13:50,710 --> 00:13:54,343
Predict what git status shows
when there's no index at all.

178
00:13:54,683 --> 00:13:59,450
Without an index, every path of HEAD looks
like a staged deletion, and every file

179
00:13:59,490 --> 00:14:04,375
looks untracked. git reset 🟡 CAUTION wrote
a new index from HEAD and left the files

180
00:14:04,415 --> 00:14:04,731
alone.

181
00:14:05,071 --> 00:14:10,323
The files are intact. What's gone is the
knowledge of which change was staged: both

182
00:14:10,363 --> 00:14:15,615
changes are unstaged now. The blob that was
staged is still in the object database:

183
00:14:15,655 --> 00:14:19,696
git fsck lists it as dangling, an
object that nothing points to.

184
00:14:21,536 --> 00:14:23,184
Five mistakes to watch for.

185
00:14:23,524 --> 00:14:27,692
Hiding a local edit with
--assume-unchanged or --skip-worktree.

186
00:14:27,732 --> 00:14:33,170
Root cause: the bit is a promise that there
is no edit; commands that list changes

187
00:14:33,210 --> 00:14:38,380
skip the entry, and commands that must
replace the file stop, or overwrite it.

188
00:14:38,720 --> 00:14:44,455
A script reports a dirty tree in a fresh
copy. Root cause: plumbing compares cached

189
00:14:44,495 --> 00:14:50,161
stat data only, and the copy has new
timestamps; run git update-index -q --refresh

190
00:14:50,201 --> 00:14:52,736
first, or use git status --porcelain.

191
00:14:53,076 --> 00:14:56,680
Removing index.lock while
a Git process is running.

192
00:14:56,720 --> 00:15:01,466
Root cause: the lock was live, not
stale; check with pgrep -fl git.

193
00:15:01,806 --> 00:15:06,988
Rebuilding the index during a merge. Root
cause: the conflict stages exist only in

194
00:15:07,028 --> 00:15:07,625
the index.

195
00:15:07,965 --> 00:15:12,718
Treating the index as storage. Root cause:
a blob that only the index refers to

196
00:15:12,758 --> 00:15:17,693
becomes unreachable the moment its entry
changes, and is pruned after two weeks by

197
00:15:17,733 --> 00:15:18,179
default.

198
00:15:20,019 --> 00:15:25,363
Now, out of the lab. A team's README tells
every developer to run --skip-worktree on

199
00:15:25,403 --> 00:15:30,682
a configuration file. The textbook names
what that creates: state that no clone and

200
00:15:30,722 --> 00:15:35,810
no CI image reproduces, and that fails at
the next upstream change to that file.

201
00:15:35,850 --> 00:15:41,065
The replacement costs one commit: an ignored
settings.local.yaml that no commit on

202
00:15:41,105 --> 00:15:46,449
any branch has ever tracked, and an application
that reads the shared defaults first

203
00:15:46,489 --> 00:15:48,436
and the local overrides second.

204
00:15:48,776 --> 00:15:53,581
And the release-build case: a build of
an unchanged commit gets labelled dirty

205
00:15:53,621 --> 00:15:58,611
inside a container or after a cache restore,
because the copy gave every file new

206
00:15:58,651 --> 00:16:03,580
stat data. The lock message in CI usually
means two Git processes in one working

207
00:16:03,620 --> 00:16:08,052
tree. Background tools should run git
--no-optional-locks status, or set

208
00:16:08,092 --> 00:16:09,356
GIT_OPTIONAL_LOCKS=0.

